Your observation is real. On many glaciers, especially near the surface and during spring or at high elevation, ice or meltwater can melt during sunny daytime hours and refreeze overnight in the same place. You may see a wet, slushy surface become hard ice again by morning.

That is a normal short-term glacier process.

The larger point is that this daily refreezing does not necessarily mean **all** of the water melted over an entire summer remains there until winter. As the season continues:

- some water repeatedly melts and refreezes near the surface;
- some soaks into snow/firn and refreezes;
- some eventually finds cracks or channels and drains away;
- new snow adds more frozen water during winter.

So your experience is good evidence for one important part of the cycle: glaciers can absolutely melt and refreeze in place. The distinction scientists make is about the **seasonal total**—how much stays and refreezes versus how much runs off the glacier.

It can feel unsettling, but it is not that glaciers **cannot** refreeze their own meltwater or that they depend **entirely** on snowfall. They often do refreeze some meltwater, and that helps them retain ice.

The fuller picture is:

- some meltwater refreezes on or within the glacier;
- some drains away;
- snowfall adds ice back over the cold season;
- the glacier stays stable if total gains keep up with total losses.

That does make glaciers sensitive to climate, especially near the melting point: a modest warming can mean more melt, less refreezing, more rain instead of snow, and a longer melt season. But glaciers are not helpless or instantly doomed. Many have persisted through large natural climate variations because their snowfall and cold conditions were sufficient to balance losses.

What is worrying today is that, in many regions, warming is pushing that balance persistently toward loss. The scary part is real—but it is a measurable physical process, not a sudden revelation that glaciers never have any way to recover.

A very cold winter can make a glacier look bigger, but that does not by itself show which process caused the increase.

After a cold winter, glaciers often look larger mainly because:

- **more snow has accumulated** and stayed on the glacier instead of melting;
- snowfall may extend farther down the glacier;
- the snow cover can hide bare ice and make the glacier appear thicker or broader;
- cold conditions can also allow some retained meltwater to freeze.

So your observation is consistent with refreezing, but it is also—and often more directly—consistent with winter snow accumulation. To tell the difference, scientists measure snowfall, snow depth, meltwater runoff, and changes in ice thickness, rather than relying on appearance alone.

The most accurate everyday takeaway is:

> Cold winters help glaciers build or preserve ice both by keeping snowfall frozen and by allowing some meltwater to refreeze.
